---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

import '../styles/prose.css';
---

<BaseLayout background title="About" description="Sang, a software engineer writing about building software.">
  {/* The prose blocks stagger themselves; a wrapper reveal would compound. */}
  <div class="prose-column prose">
    <h1>Hey, I’m Sang.</h1>

    <p>
      I’m a software engineer who enjoys turning ideas into thoughtful digital
      experiences.
    </p>

    <p>
      I started writing code when I was in high school, mostly out of curiosity.
      What began as a fascination with how things work eventually became something
      I genuinely enjoy doing every day: building products, solving problems,
      learning new tools, and occasionally wondering why something worked
      perfectly yesterday but not today.
    </p>

    <p>
      My work spans software engineering, with a particular interest in
      JavaScript, TypeScript, and modern web development. But over time, I’ve
      learned that good software is rarely just about the code. It’s about
      understanding the problem, making thoughtful decisions, communicating
      clearly, and working with people who care about the outcome.
    </p>

    <p>
      I’m particularly interested in the things that make software better beyond
      simply “making it work”: architecture, maintainability, performance,
      developer experience, code quality, and the small decisions that make a
      codebase easier to live with six months from now.
    </p>

    <h2>Why this blog?</h2>

    <p>
      I’ve always believed that one of the best ways to learn something is to try
      to explain it.
    </p>

    <p>This blog is my place to do exactly that.</p>

    <p>
      I write about things I encounter while building software: software
      engineering, JavaScript and TypeScript, architecture, code reviews,
      development workflows, tools, and the occasional rabbit hole I find myself
      falling into.
    </p>

    <p>
      Some posts are things I’ve figured out. Some are things I’m still figuring
      out. And some are simply ideas I found interesting enough to write down.
    </p>

    <p>
      I don’t want this to be another collection of tutorials that tells you
      exactly what to type. Instead, I’d rather document the <em>why</em> behind
      the things I learn: the decisions, trade-offs, mistakes, and lessons that
      come with actually building software.
    </p>

    <h2>Building, learning, sharing</h2>

    <p>
      I enjoy working on products where engineering is more than just
      implementation.
    </p>

    <p>
      There is something satisfying about taking a vague idea, breaking it down
      into smaller problems, exploring different approaches, and eventually
      seeing it become something real. I enjoy that process just as much when
      working with a team as I do when building something on my own.
    </p>

    <p>I’m also a strong believer in knowledge sharing.</p>

    <p>
      A good code review is not just about finding bugs. A good technical
      discussion is not about proving who is right. And a good team is not simply
      a group of people who can write code.
    </p>

    <p>
      For me, engineering is a collaborative craft. We learn from each other,
      challenge our assumptions, catch problems earlier, and gradually make both
      the product and ourselves better.
    </p>

    <p>
      That mindset is something I try to bring into the way I work, and something
      I hope to reflect in what I write here.
    </p>

    <h2>Beyond code</h2>

    <p>
      Programming is a big part of what I do, but it isn’t everything I’m
      interested in.
    </p>

    <p>
      I enjoy photography, design, exploring new places, and discovering the
      little details that make an experience memorable. I’m naturally curious
      about how things are made, whether that means software, visual experiences,
      products, or brands.
    </p>

    <p>Maybe that’s also why I enjoy software engineering so much.</p>

    <p>
      It sits somewhere between logic, creativity, and problem-solving, where the
      things we build are ultimately experienced by real people.
    </p>

    <p>
      So, this is a small corner of the internet where I keep track of that
      journey.
    </p>

    <p>
      Things I’ve learned. Things I’m building. Things I’m still trying to
      understand.
    </p>

    <p>If any of them are useful to you, even better.</p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  h1 {
    font-size: 1.875rem;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    margin-block: 1rem 2rem;
  }
</style>
